WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:08.440
When we are in a stationary car that moves rapidly to gain speed,

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.700
we get pushed back against our seat.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.480
And when brakes are applied, we get pushed ahead.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.540
Or when we take a sharp right turn, we are pushed towards the left.

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.480
These are all situations where we are Accelerating!

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.280
We have all experienced this whether in a car,

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.280
or a bus, or while riding a motorbike.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.420
Simply put, when velocity changes, we have acceleration.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.540
I still haven't defined what acceleration is.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:42.040
I just told you that when velocity changes, there will be acceleration.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.080
Okay, let me give you two examples.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:46.040
In the first one,

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:51.380
a car is traveling in a straight line at a constant speed of 80 kilometers an hour.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:52.640
And in the second one,

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.960
a plane is traveling in a straight line at a constant speed

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:58.380
of 1,000 kilometers an hour.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:03.040
In which case do you think Acceleration is involved?

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:12.060
None! In both cases, the velocity is not changing at all.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.140
Hence, there will be no acceleration in either of the cases!

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.520
Most people relate acceleration to high speed !

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:21.760
And that's clearly not true!

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:26.200
Acceleration will only exist when there is change in velocity.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.120
Do you remember what velocity is ?

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.820
Its speed with direction and its hence of vector quantity!

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.060
Why is this important?

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:37.760
Let's say a body moves from rest and

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:42.180
reaches a velocity of 20 kilometers an hour in five seconds.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.280
Will acceleration be involved here?

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:50.200
Yes, because the velocity is changing. It was zero initially,

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.240
and at the end of five seconds it's 20 kilometers per hour.

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:55.970
In another scenario,

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:58.960
assume that a body is moving at 30 kilometers an hour,

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.480
and then it moves right at 30 kilometers an hour

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:07.080
and continues traveling at the same speed of 30 kilometers per hour.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:10.580
Will there be acceleration in this case?

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:16.640
The answer is yes !

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:18.840
As the direction is changing,

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.180
the velocity will also change.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:24.800
The only thing constant in this example is speed.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:28.820
But the velocity changes as the direction changes!

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:33.180
So this was the most important concept you had to know about acceleration.

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:36.840
It will exist only when there is a change in velocity!

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:40.340
But how is acceleration calculated?

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:45.080
Acceleration is defined as the rate of change of velocity.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:46.780
In terms of a formula,

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.560
Acceleration can be written as 'change in velocity'

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:53.680
'over the time taken'. If the velocity of an object changes

00:02:53.900 --> 00:03:00.660
from an initial value of U to the final value of V, in time t, then the acceleration

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.680
"a" can be written as 'V minus U' over t.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.620
Said this object is at rest initially at point "a".

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.260
It then travels to the right and attains the velocity of

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:14.980
'10 meters per second' in five seconds.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:18.440
What will be the acceleration here at point B?

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:21.740
We can look at the formula to get the answer!

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.320
The initial velocity U is zero meters per second as the object is stationary.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:30.640
The final Velocity V is 10 meters per second.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.740
And the time taken t is 5 seconds.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:37.960
Hence the acceleration a will equal '10 meters per second' minus

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:41.560
'zero meters per second', divided by 5 seconds.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:45.980
So this will equal 10 meters per second divided by 5 seconds.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:48.460
'10 divided by 5' is 2.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.640
Wait.. hold on..

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:53.560
What will be the units of acceleration?

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:55.640
We should get a hint by looking at this.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.040
The units of acceleration will be

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.100
'meters per second' per second.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:05.040
Because it's the change in velocity over the time taken!

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:07.300
As this is meters per second,

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.460
we can carry this second to the denominator

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:13.200
and what we will get is 'meters per second squared.'

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:17.960
This one is more intuitive, but this one is a bit neater I would say.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:23.540
So the acceleration in this case will be '2 meters per second squared'.

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:26.340
And acceleration is also a vector quantity!

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:27.680
Here,

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.560
Acceleration and Velocity are in the same direction!

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.520
There are many more important concepts

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:37.900
about acceleration, which we will cover in the coming videos.

